WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.292 --> 00:00:05.250
SPEAKER: We understand
that retail banks depend

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.740
on Temenos apps to
process essential banking

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:12.060
transactions, from deposits and
withdrawals to critical loans.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.030
In this demo, our fictional
bank has deployed their Transact

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.840
core banking app running on
new OTP distributed database

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.240
on Anthos Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.930
giving them the flexibility
to run production workloads

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.510
on premises in their
own data center

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.250
while also having
a secondary site

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.590
on Google Cloud
set up for bursting

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.840
to handle unforeseen
surges in core banking

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.980
transactions, failover to the
cloud for disaster recovery,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.820
and autoscaling back down to
maintain business as usual.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.280
This full demo, if done
live, spans one hour,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.560
so keep in mind that we
have edited for time.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.440
Let's take a quick
tour of the dashboard.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.680
Starting on the upper left, you
can see the ActiveMQ message

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:55.940
queue count.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.665
Going down the left side, you
see the Transact and NuoDB CPU

00:00:59.665 --> 00:01:03.500
and NuoDB commits
on-prem and in the cloud.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.280
We're using JMeter to load the
transactions into ActiveMQ.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.380
So on the upper right
chart, we present the number

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.660
of transactions per second.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.490
In this demo, the
system on-prem is

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.410
built to handle a
maximum normal load

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.450
of 20 transactions per second.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.470
Going down the right side, see
the two GKE nodes ready to go,

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.970
with the ability to
autoscale up to five nodes.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.150
Transact on premise is set
scale up to three replicas.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.280
After that, to support
additional load,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.580
Transact is set to scale up
to 19 replicas in Google Cloud

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.250
GKE.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.710
And on the bottom, we show
how NuoDB Transaction Engine

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.818
is also set to scale
up to three replicas.

00:01:42.818 --> 00:01:44.360
As we've been going
through the tour,

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:47.090
you can see that, with the
exception of a brief spike,

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.620
the system has been
processing a fairly steady

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.490
and, quote unquote, "normal
load" of about 13 transactions

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.730
per second.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.800
In the ActiveMQ
chart, you can also

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.020
see that, for the most
part, transactions

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.440
are being processed right away.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.800
So messages are not queueing up.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.490
All looks good so far.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.050
We've started fast
forwarding, so you

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.930
can see how Google Cloud
can help with a sudden surge

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.650
up to 150 transactions
per second and beyond.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.400
GKE Transact replica
scaled to 19,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.050
and GKE nodes autoscale up to 5.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.970
NuoDB transaction
engine will also

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.250
scale up to 2, with
the CPU utilization

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.170
rate exceeding the target.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.480
Messages are building
in the queue.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.000
We fast forward more to show
how the system is coping well

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.910
with the increasingly heavy
load, and that activity

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:38.580
in the NuoDB transaction
engine replicas is converging.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.450
Finally, we reach peak activity
of about 250 transactions

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.740
per second.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.230
Messages really start
accumulating as just too

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.360
much for the system to handle.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.310
Now, we simulate a
worst case scenario

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:54.810
in this demo, failure on
premises at the maximum load.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.610
In this DR scenario,
Cloud is just

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.490
about managing the messages,
while the on-prem site is down.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.130
After about 470
seconds of downtime,

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.310
the on-prem data center
is operational again.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.770
Next, we'll show how
transact on Google Cloud

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:15.740
is set to automatically scale
back down when transaction

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.930
loads return to normal.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.270
Messages have gone down to zero,
transaction engine activity

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.540
converges, and Transact
replicas in Google Cloud scale

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.830
back to 1.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.990
And NuoDB transaction
engine in the cloud

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:29.160
also scales down to 2.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.880
The system in the cloud
continues to scale down.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.400
NuoDB transaction
engine replicas

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.520
are now down to a minimum.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:47.620
And the GKE nodes are starting
to scale back down from 5 to 2

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.160
as well.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.590
In conclusion, this
demo showed how

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.640
Transact can be set up to burst
seamlessly from on premises

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.990
to Google Cloud to handle
unforeseen spikes in load,

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.510
how we can failover
to the cloud,

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.860
and also autoscale
back down again

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.150
to maintain business as usual.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.090
Thank you for watching.

